\end{center}

\section{Discussion}

A congruence $\sigma_j\equiv\sigma_k\pmod m$ is equivalent to the pointwise divisibility $m\mid a^j-a^k$ for
all $a$. The latter is a finite condition, and the optimal modulus is the computable gcd
$M_{j,k} = \gcd_{a\in\Z}(a^j-a^k)$: for $(7,3)$ it equals $120$, for $(5,3)$ it equals $24$, both realized at
$a=2$. This dissolves the mystery of the constant $120$: it is the gcd of the polynomial family $a^7-a^3$. The
identity $E_4^2=E_8$ explains \emph{why} $\sigma_7-\sigma_3$ is a $120$-fold convolution; the transfer
principle explains \emph{that} it is a multiple of $120$ and pins the constant sharply.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Exact convolution law]
For all $n\ge 1$, $\displaystyle \sigma_7(n) = \sigma_3(n) + 120\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\sigma_3(i)\sigma_3(n-i)$.
\end{conjecture}
This upgrades \eqref{eq:cong} to an equality equivalent to $E_4^2=E_8$; a purely elementary
(Liouville-style) proof would complete it for all $n$.

\begin{conjecture}[Congruence hierarchy across even weights]
For each odd exponent $2k-1$ there is an optimal modulus $M_k = \gcd_a(a^{2k-1}-a^3)$ with
$\sigma_{2k-1}(n)\equiv\sigma_3(n)\pmod{M_k}$, governed by the denominators of Bernoulli numbers. The instances
$M=24$ (exponent $5$) and $M=120$ (exponent $7$) are proved sharp.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Lattice-genus congruences]
For every even unimodular lattice of rank $16$, the number of vectors of squared length $2n$ is congruent
modulo $28800$ to $240\,\sigma_3(n)$; the lattices $E_8\oplus E_8$ and $D_{16}^+$ differ only through a
cusp-form contribution vanishing modulo $28800$.
\end{conjecture}

